","mTOR inhibitors are a class of drugs that target the mechanistic-target kinase C (mTOR) pathway, which plays a crucial role in cell growth, proliferation, and survival. mTOR is a serine kinase that is part of two complexes, mTOR-1 and mTOR-2, each with distinct roles. mTOR inhibitors are primarily designed to target mTOR-1, as it is more implicated in cancer and other disease-related processes.

mTOR inhibitors are used in cancer treatment, and also in treating rare genetic disorders like spinal muscular atrophy. They are also studied for their potential benefits in treating other conditions like cardiovascular diseases and metabolic disorders.

mTOR inhibitors are classified into two main classes: inhibitors of mTOR-1 and inhibitors of both mTOR-1 and mTOR-2. For example, raplitinib inhibits both mTOR-1 and mTOR-2, while rapatinib inhibits only mTOR-1.

mTOR inhibitors are designed to inhibit the activity of mTOR, which prevents the activation of downstream signaling pathways that promote cell growth and proliferation. This can help to slow or kill cancer cells, as well as to protect the function of healthy cells in other diseases.

