What is the definition of whole body oxygen delivery, and how is it calculated?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The definition of whole body oxygen delivery is the rate at which oxygen is delivered to the body's tissues. It is a measure of the body's ability to provide oxygen to the cells needed for energy production and survival. A healthy oxygen delivery rate ensures that the body's metabolic processes can function efficiently.

Oxygen delivery is calculated using the following formula:

Oxygen Delivery (OD) = VO2 × VO2/CO × CO2

where:
- VO2 = volume of oxygen consumed per minute (ml/min)
- VO2/CO = oxygen-to-carbon dioxide ratio (ml/min)
- CO2 = carbon dioxide production per minute (ml/min)

This formula is derived from the mass-mass relationship, which states that the amount of oxygen consumed is equal to the amount of carbon dioxide produced during cellular respiration.

