   - Anti-inflammatory action: NSAIDs inhibit the production of prostaglandins, which are involved in the development of inflammation. By inhibiting prostaglandins, NSAIDs can help to reduce inflammation symptoms.

In summary, the primary mechanism of action of NSAIDs is the inhibition of COX enzymes, which reduces the production of prostaglandins, prostaglandins-like molecules (e.g., leukokinotropines), and thromoglangin. This inhibition leads to their therapeutic effects, including antipyretic, analgesic, and anti-inflammatory actions.
