</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To calculate the change in entropy (ΔS) for the reaction, we can use the following equation:

ΔS = Σn_f * ΔS_f(products) - Σn_r * ΔS_f(reactants)

where n_f and n_r are the stoichiometric coefficients of the products and reactants, respectively, and ΔS_f is the standard entropy of formation.

For the reaction:

3 moles of CO (g) + O2 (g) → 3 moles of CO2 (g)

We need the standard entropy of formation (ΔS_f) values for each compound at 298 K:

ΔS_f(CO) = 135.6 J/mol·K
ΔS_f(O2) = 205.7 J/mol·K
ΔS_f(CO2) = 239.1 J/mol·K

Now we can plug these values into the equation:

ΔS = [3 * ΔS_f(CO2)] - [3 * ΔS_f(CO) + 1 * ΔS_f(O2)]

ΔS = [3 * 239.1 J/mol·K] - [3 * 135.6 J/mol·K + 1 * 205.7 J/mol·K]

ΔS = 717.3 J/mol·K - [406.8 J/mol·K + 205.7 J/mol·K]

ΔS = 717.3 J/mol·K - 612.5 J/mol·K

ΔS = 104.8 J/mol·K

The change in entropy for the reaction is 104.8 J/mol·K.
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

