","1. **Calculate the molality of naphthalene solution:**
   \[
   \text{Molality} = \frac{\text{Moles of solute}}{\text{Mass of solvent (kg)}}
   \]
   \[
   \text{Moles of naphthalene} = \frac{0.5126 \text{ g}}{128.17 \text{ g/mol}} = 0.00401 \text{ mol}
   \]
   \[
   \text{Mass of CCl₄ (kg)} = 50 \text{ g} = 0.050 \text{ kg}
   \]
   \[
   \text{Molality of naphthalene solution} = \frac{0.00401 \text{ mol}}{0.050 \text{ kg}} = 0.0802 \text{ mol/kg}
   \]

2. **Calculate the molality of the unknown solute solution:**
   \[
   \text{Moles of unknown solute} = \frac{0.6216 \text{ g}}{x \text{ g/mol}} = \frac{0.00401 \text{ mol}}{128.17 \text{ g/mol}}
   \]
   \[
   \text{Molality of unknown solute solution} = \frac{0.6216 \text{ g}}{0.050 \text{ kg}} = 12.43 \text{ mol/kg}
   \]

3. **Use the boiling point elevation equation:**
   \[
   \Delta T = K_b \cdot m
   \]
   where:
   - \(\Delta T\) is the boiling point elevation
   - \(K_b\) is the boiling point elevation constant of the solvent
   - \(m\) is the molality of the solution

   For CCl₄, \(K_b = 0.0005 \text{ °C/mol/kg}\).

   \[
   0.402 = 0.0005 \times 0.0802 \implies \text{This is incorrect, as the molality is not constant.}
   \]
   \[
   0.647 = 0.0005 \times 12.43 \implies \text{This is also incorrect, as the molality is not constant.}
   \]
   The correct answer is:
   \[
   \Delta T = K_b \times m \implies m = \frac{\Delta T}{K_b}
   \]
