","When \( \text{H}_2\text{S} \) gas is passed into a mixture of \( \text{Mn}^{2+}, \text{Ni}^{2+}, \text{Cu}^{2+}, \) and \( \text{Hg}^{2+} \) ions in an acidified aqueous solution, the sulfides that precipitate are determined by the solubility product (\( K_{sp} \)) of their corresponding sulfides. The \( K_{p} \) values for the sulfides are:  
- \( \text{MnS} \): \( K_{sp} = 1.4 \times 10^{-10} \)  
- \( \text{NiS} \): \( K_{sp} = 1.0 \times 10^{-10} \)  
- \( \text{CuS} \): \( K_{sp} = 1.0 \times 10^{-10} \)  
- \( \text{HgS} \): \( K_{sp} = 1.0 \times 10^{-10} \)  

Since the \( K_{sp} \) values for \( \text{NiS} \), \( \text{CuS} \), and \( \text{HgS} \) are the same, they will precipitate at the same concentration of \( \text{H}_2\text{S} \). The \( K_{sp} \) value for \( \text{MgS} \) is larger than the others, so it will not precipitate.  

Thus, the sulfides that precipitate are:  
- \( \text{NiS} \)  
- \( \text{CuS} \)  
- \( \text{HgS} \)  

The correct answer is \(\boxed{\text{NiS, CuS, HgS}}\).",ground_truth_similarity,"# Task Description
