   - The problem assumes the best-case scenario, where the pivot divides the input list into two equal-length sublists of \( n/2 \) elements each.
   - For example, if \( n = 4 \), the two sublists would have 2 elements each. If \( n = 6 \), the two sublists would have 3 elements each.

