(b) How long does it take the proton to reach a speed of \(35,000 \, \text{m/s}\)?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","(a) The force on the proton is given by \(F = qE\), where \(q\) is the charge and \(E\) is the electric field strength. Substituting the given values:  
\[
F = (1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C})(500 \, \text{N/C}) = 8.0 \times 10^{-17} \, \text{N}.
\]  
Using Newton's second law, \(F = ma\), we can find the acceleration:  
\[
a = \frac{F}{m} = \frac{8.0 \times 10^{-17} \, \text{N}}{1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}} = 4.8 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2.
\]  
Thus, the magnitude of the acceleration is \(\boxed{4.8 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2}\).

(b) Using the kinematic equation \(v = u + at\), where \(v\) is the final velocity, \(u\) is the initial velocity, \(a\) is the acceleration, and \(t\) is the time, we can find the time it takes to reach a speed of \(35,000 \, \text{m/s}\). Since the proton starts from rest, \(u = 0\).  
\[
t = \frac{v - u}{a} = \frac{35,000 \, \text{m/s} - 0}{4.8 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2} = 7.2 \times 10^{-5} \, \text{s}.
\]  
Thus, the time it takes to reach a speed of \(35,000 \, \text{m/s}\) is \(\boxed{7.2 \times 10^{-5} \, \text{s}}\).",relevance,"# Task Description
