The decay length (\(L\)) of an evanescent wave is given by:  
\[
L = \frac{1}{k_0 \left( n_{\text{eff}} - n_f \right)}
\]  
where:  
- \(k_0\) is the free-index \(k\)-value (refractive index of free medium)  
- \(n_{\text{eff}} = 1.05\) is the refractive index of the evanescent wave  
- \(n_f = 1.00\) is the refractive index of the surrounding medium (air)  

Substituting the given values:  
- \(n_{\text{eff}} - n_f = 0.3\)  
- \(k_0 = \frac{c}{\lambda} = \frac{3 \times 10^8 \, \text{m/s}}{1 \, \mu\text{m}} = 3 \times 10^8 \, \text{rad/m}\)  

\[
L = \frac{1}{3 \times 10^8 \, \text{rad/m} \times 0.3} = \frac{1}{9 \times 10^7 \, \text{rad/m}} \approx 1.11 \times 10^{-8} \, \text{m} = 11.1 \, \mu\text{m}
\]  

Thus, the decay length is \(\boxed{11.1 \, \mu\text{m}}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

