An amplifier has a voltage gain \( A_v = 100 \) without feedback. A fraction \( m_v = 0.1 \) of the output is fed back in opposition to the input (negative feedback). If the gain \( A_v \) falls by 6 dB due to ageing, calculate the percentage change in the closed-loop gain \( A_{vf} \).<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The closed-loop voltage gain \( A_{vf} \) is given by:
\[
A_{vf} = \frac{A_v}{1 + m_v A_v}
\]
Substituting the given values:
\[
A_{vf} = \frac{100}{1 + 0.1 \times 100} = \frac{100}{1 + 10} = \frac{100}{11} \approx 9.09 \text{ V/V}
\]
The gain \( A_v \) decreases by 6 dB due to ageing. The new gain \( A_v' \) is:
\[
A_v' = \frac{A_v}{2} = \frac{100}{2} = 50 \text{ V/V}
\]
The new closed-loop voltage gain \( A_{vf}' \) is:
\[
A_{vf}' = \frac{A_v'}{1 + m_v A_v'} = \frac{50}{1 + 0.1 \times 50} = \frac{50}{1 + 5} = \frac{50}{6} \approx 8.33 \text{ V/V}
\]
The percentage change in the closed-loop gain is:
\[
\text{Percentage change} = \left( \frac{A_{vf}' - A_{vf}}{A_{vf}} \right) \times 100 = \left( \frac{8.33 - 9.09}{9.09} \right) \times 100 \approx \left( \frac{-0.76}{9.09} \right) \times 100 \approx -8.33\%
\]
Thus, the percentage change in the closed-loop gain is approximately \(\boxed{-8.33\%}\).",fluency,"# Task Description
