","The maximum percentage change in the wavelength of a 2.00 Å photon scattered by an electron is given by the Compton scattering formula:  
\[
\Delta \lambda = \lambda \left( \frac{2h}{m_e c} \right)
\]  
where:  
- \(\Delta \lambda\) is the change in wavelength,  
- \(\lambda\) is the original wavelength,  
- \(h = 6.626 \times 10^{-34} \, \text{J s}\) is Planck's constant,  
- \(m_e = 9.11 \times 10^{-31} \, \text{kg}\) is the mass of the electron,  
- \(c = 3 \times 10^8 \, \text{m s}^{-1}\) is the speed of light.  

Substituting the values:  
\[
\frac{2h}{m_e c} = \frac{2 \times 6.626 \times 10^{-34}}{9.11 \times 10^{-31} \times 3 \times 10^8} = 1.16 \times 10^{-10}
\]  

The percentage change in wavelength is:  
\[
\frac{\Delta \lambda}{\lambda} \times 100 = 1.16 \times 10^{-10} \times 100 = 1.16 \times 10^{-8}
\]  
Thus, the maximum percentage change in the wavelength is \(\boxed{1.16 \times 10^{-8}}\).",fluency,"# Task Description
